WEBVTT


00:00:01.631 --> 00:00:07.631
Hello, welcome back to our course, today we are going to talk about string handling in R.

00:00:17.936 --> 00:00:20.936
Alright, so strings are often a natural first step to save information, strings are text and numbers combined, and it's often the easiest

00:00:22.671 --> 00:00:25.671
to get data from external sources via strings.

00:00:25.748 --> 00:00:33.748
Strings are entered using this double quotation mark or single quotation mark, and here you can see that I'm defining the first string.

00:00:39.961 --> 00:00:42.961
And my first string is these two characters: "Hello42". In the second string, I have something which is longer.

00:00:42.556 --> 00:00:53.556
And then inside, I have a quotation mark. So basically, I start with a single quotation mark, and then inside, if I would like to include a quote,

00:00:57.269 --> 00:01:00.269
then I could use a double quotation mark.

00:01:05.090 --> 00:01:09.090
So, if we would like to concatenate strings or glue them together, then we use this function paste.

00:01:12.238 --> 00:01:18.238
Paste takes arbitrary number of arguments and concatenates them. So, as a default

00:01:20.530 --> 00:01:24.530
a separator between two different elements is this space,

00:01:30.171 --> 00:01:32.171
But we could also change it. For example, here we have two elements, A and B, and I would like to paste them.

00:01:37.734 --> 00:01:41.734
If I paste them, then I obtain "Hello 42". If I paste them, but I change a separator, then I have "Hello,42"

00:01:46.021 --> 00:01:49.021
And then a subfunction is paste0.

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:57.000
So here, basically, I glue elements together, but without any space. So you can see that X

00:01:57.685 --> 00:02:02.685
is glued to 1, and Y is glued to 4 without any space.

00:02:02.730 --> 00:02:11.730
And then you can see that also a paste function recycles certain elements. For example, here, we had only two

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:19.080
letters, X and Y, but full numbers. So we have X1, Y2, X3, and Y4.

00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:27.220
Here, let us define this identity matrix, C, and then, if we apply paste to c,

00:02:31.251 --> 00:02:34.251
then it spells out all the elements of that identity matrix.

00:02:34.935 --> 00:02:44.935
But then, if we apply here to that function collapse parameter, then you can see that we have all these elements, but without any quotation mark.

00:02:45.777 --> 00:02:54.777
We could also manipulate our strings, so how can we do it? Well, we could apply a function toupper().

00:02:59.831 --> 00:03:05.831
toupper() makes all the characters uppercase letter. And then, tolower() makes all the characters

00:03:05.707 --> 00:03:09.707
lowercase letters. So here you can see that we have all lowercase.

00:03:12.608 --> 00:03:16.608
We could also substitute certain characters in a string. So, for example, using this function chartr(),

00:03:21.027 --> 00:03:25.027
we select an old character and the new, and then you can see that instead of banana,

00:03:24.386 --> 00:03:29.386
we have Bonono, and then instead of a pineapple, we have

00:03:34.388 --> 00:03:40.388
Pineopple. We can also count how many characters there are in a particular

00:03:43.187 --> 00:03:46.187
string, so here you can see that the first element, we have 6 characters, and then in the second one, we have 9.

00:03:51.232 --> 00:03:53.232
And then, substr() extracts a substring. So, basically, here you can see that

00:04:01.444 --> 00:04:04.444
I would like to extract the substring, which starts at the second place and stops in the fourth. So, in case of the first element of our vector,

00:04:04.056 --> 00:04:10.056
the second character is A, the fourth is A, so we subtracted

00:04:18.344 --> 00:04:22.344
Here: "ana". And similar for the second element. Alright, so now let us briefly talk about regular expressions.

00:04:20.362 --> 00:04:27.362
Regular expressions are these patterns that describe a set of strings. So, for example, here the pattern

00:04:27.355 --> 00:04:34.355
Is ea, and then if I would like to find ea in this poem, then it appears in beauty,

00:04:38.387 --> 00:04:40.387
increases and Keats. And there are different types of regular expressions in R.

00:04:40.690 --> 00:04:46.690
We have this POSIX extended expressions and Perl-like, but basically, think about that as:

00:04:51.460 --> 00:04:54.460
How can I find a substring in a particular string? And there are different commands to do it.

00:04:55.160 --> 00:05:05.160
And then, regular expressions can be passed into many basic functions in R, and we are going to look

00:05:05.656 --> 00:05:11.656
into all of them, and also these are used in other functions, and here you have a link with a

00:05:15.696 --> 00:05:19.696
cheat sheet for regular expressions.

00:05:24.566 --> 00:05:26.566
If we would like to detect in which line a particular expression appears, we could use a function grep().

00:05:32.340 --> 00:05:36.340
greb(), it's used in the following way: So, we paste

00:05:42.223 --> 00:05:45.223
an expression we are looking for, and we paste our object here, we have this vector composed of four elements, and all of them are these characters.

00:05:48.711 --> 00:05:50.711
And then you can see that ea appears in the first element, first line,

00:06:02.060 --> 00:06:05.060
second element, second line, and fourth line. So these four. grepl() returns a logical vector. So basically, here we have four elements as an output, and then the first is true,

00:06:11.765 --> 00:06:12.765
because ea appears in the first element, in the second also true.

00:06:12.086 --> 00:06:17.086
But it doesn't appear in the third element, third line, so this is false.

00:06:17.197 --> 00:06:24.197
We have also functions to locate a particular pattern, so this function regexpr()

00:06:25.002 --> 00:06:30.002
returns the position of ea in the lines, and minus 1 indicates no match.

00:06:33.022 --> 00:06:36.022
So here, if we once again look at our poem and we apply the function regexpr()

00:06:36.392 --> 00:06:41.392
and we are looking for ea, what do we get? We get 13, so this is

00:06:43.911 --> 00:06:49.911
indicating us that ea appears on the place 13, 20 in the second line,

00:06:49.262 --> 00:06:55.262
Minus 1 here, third line. Why? Because in the third line, we didn't have ea.

00:07:01.027 --> 00:07:05.027
And then position 9 in the fourth line. We also have this attribute here. So the attribute match.length

00:07:05.644 --> 00:07:11.644
tells us what is the length of the expression we are looking for, and the length is 2.

00:07:11.057 --> 00:07:18.057
But in the third line, we didn't have it, so we can see minus 1 appearing.

00:07:22.859 --> 00:07:24.859
Another function 

00:07:23.242 --> 00:07:32.242
gregexpr() is useful if there are multiple matches in one line, so it returns as a list for each line

00:07:32.914 --> 00:07:38.914
of text containing all match positions in a vector. So now, if I apply it to our poem,

00:07:38.185 --> 00:07:43.185
and now, I would like to see only the results for the second line,

00:07:47.420 --> 00:07:49.420
you can see that in the second line, there are two appearances of es:

00:07:49.339 --> 00:07:56.339
On the 12th place and 23rd place. And then here, the match.length

00:08:00.456 --> 00:08:02.456
attribute gives us 2 and 2, because the length of these

00:08:08.495 --> 00:08:12.495
two patterns is two. Now, as you can see,

00:08:17.425 --> 00:08:21.425
ea is a fixed, exact string, so it's faster to actually

00:08:25.974 --> 00:08:30.974
specify another parameter in grep() and grepl() to specify fixed to true. In this case, these functions run faster,

00:08:35.392 --> 00:08:38.392
but these functions could be also used to look for non-constant patterns:

00:08:42.616 --> 00:08:46.616
Meta characters, character classes, and quantifiers. What do I mean by non-constant? So imagine that we are looking for

00:08:48.269 --> 00:08:52.269
a pattern which starts with O, but then the second

00:08:52.258 --> 00:09:00.258
letter is just lowercase letter. So here, we will have a few matches: We would have of,

00:09:03.509 --> 00:09:05.509
oy, or,

00:09:08.033 --> 00:09:11.033
ov in loveliness, and oh

00:09:17.979 --> 00:09:20.979
in John, oh, and also ot in nothingness. And there are different types of meta-characters. For example, we have anchors:

00:09:28.426 --> 00:09:30.426
The first symbol, caret. It starts a string, so basically indicates us

00:09:31.124 --> 00:09:34.124
where the string should start, how it should start,

00:09:40.842 --> 00:09:42.842
and the dollar sign how it should end. An example. Here we have an example with a vector X and a few elements.

00:09:45.101 --> 00:09:49.101
And then, if I apply now a function grepl(), and then I ask, hey, find me

00:09:56.197 --> 00:10:00.197
an element which starts, so a caret, with "cat". The first element, okay, this is true, because indeed, cat starts with cat.

00:10:00.129 --> 00:10:06.129
Then, scat. Scat doesn't start with cat, so we get false.

00:10:07.018 --> 00:10:12.018
But catfish indeed starts with cat, so we get true, etc.

00:10:17.040 --> 00:10:20.040
Now, we are looking for those elements which end with "cat". So what do we have here? We have this:

00:10:20.256 --> 00:10:26.256
True for the first element, because we have cat.

00:10:26.495 --> 00:10:30.495
We have true for the second element, because we have scat,

00:10:35.691 --> 00:10:37.691
but we have false for the, third element, because we have catfish.

00:10:37.152 --> 00:10:43.152
And then we have, once again, true for the fourth element, because we have a cat.

00:10:45.142 --> 00:10:50.142
And similarly for other elements.

00:10:54.207 --> 00:10:59.207
Another type of meta-characters are groups of and alternation. So a group

00:11:09.404 --> 00:11:11.404
is basically capturing group. So, we sort of evaluate it first, and then we also have this vertical bar, which is alternation, so basically, we are looking for something, OR something else.

00:11:20.512 --> 00:11:25.512
An example. So here, we would like to capture this "ha" three times, so instead of writing ha ha ha, I just

00:11:31.940 --> 00:11:33.940
wrote in "ha" in the parentheses, so it means that this is a capturing group. So basically, I would like to capture that entire group 3 times.

00:11:34.134 --> 00:11:38.134
So you can see that, we defined that vector A,

00:11:38.593 --> 00:11:46.593
and now I would like to capture "ha" 3 times in A. So, the first element here as an output is true, because we have ha-ha-ha.

00:11:47.028 --> 00:11:51.028
Then, the second element here is false, because we have ha only twice.

00:11:51.568 --> 00:11:59.568
And then the third element, it's also false, because we have ha 3 times, but with a space, and we are looking for something which was only ha 3 times.

00:12:00.424 --> 00:12:05.424
Now, when it comes to or expressions, so here we have another vector, B,

00:12:07.506 --> 00:12:09.506
and then I'm looking for "cat or dog".

00:12:09.762 --> 00:12:14.762
Clearly, I have a cat in a catfish, so I have true.

00:12:14.167 --> 00:12:20.167
I have a dog in a hot dog, so I have true as well, but I don't have

00:12:20.904 --> 00:12:25.904
either cat or fish in a hamster, so I have false here.

00:12:30.860 --> 00:12:34.860
Alright, and we also have character classes. So, character classes here, we have 3 different types. We have

00:12:35.078 --> 00:12:42.078
this character class in a bracket, ABC, so what does it mean? So I'm looking for any one

00:12:47.618 --> 00:12:49.618
of A, B, or C. Then, with a caret, I'm looking for any character except

00:12:52.807 --> 00:12:55.807
A, B, or C. So, caret here negates only the very first character after the bracket

00:12:55.249 --> 00:12:59.249
and then the dot is in any character except a new line.

00:13:04.711 --> 00:13:06.711
So once again, an example. So we have grepl(), we are looking for ABC in that character class,

00:13:11.919 --> 00:13:15.919
and then, in that particular vector. So as an output, we get true, because we have A in A.

00:13:16.410 --> 00:13:20.410
The second element is true as well, because we have B in B.

00:13:20.508 --> 00:13:26.508
And the third element is true, because we have C in cup, but the fourth element is false,

00:13:26.433 --> 00:13:31.433
because we don't have either A or B or C in dog.

00:13:36.149 --> 00:13:40.149
Now, when it comes to negating, remember, it only negates the very first character. So, now I'm looking for something which has

00:13:48.517 --> 00:13:51.517
not A, not B or C as a first character. Okay, so, as an output, I get false, because I have A here,

00:13:51.793 --> 00:13:57.793
the second one is ABC. It's also false, because I have A.

00:13:58.104 --> 00:14:07.104
The third element is false, because I have C, and I was looking for not A or not B or not C.

00:14:09.907 --> 00:14:11.907
But the fourth element is true, because D

00:14:15.976 --> 00:14:19.976
Is not A or B or C. So, indeed, I get true. And similarly for the last element

00:14:20.090 --> 00:14:26.090
and dot any character except new lines, so basically here, I'm looking for any character.

00:14:26.693 --> 00:14:30.693
Okay, this is true for A, because A is any character.

00:14:31.031 --> 00:14:34.031
This is false here, because I don't have anything, that's like

00:14:41.734 --> 00:14:44.734
emptiness, so, this is false. But it's true, in case of C, because C, lowercase c is also any character.

00:14:45.719 --> 00:14:51.719
Okay, and we also have quantifiers. So, quantifiers tell us how many times something should appear.

00:14:55.052 --> 00:14:58.052
So we have asterisks, that should appear zero or more times, plus

00:15:01.112 --> 00:15:03.112
one or more times, question mark. 0 or 1 time, and this

00:15:03.365 --> 00:15:08.365
M and N in the curly bracket, so the preceding element should be

00:15:08.941 --> 00:15:17.941
in the string at least m, but not more than n times. Okay, so let us look at the examples. So we have asterisks zero or more times.

00:15:18.062 --> 00:15:24.062
And then, here in this expression, what do I have? I should have something which starts with h.

00:15:26.777 --> 00:15:31.777
And then it has zero or more A's, and it should end that way.

00:15:31.746 --> 00:15:38.746
Okay, so H. This is true. Why? Because it starts with H, and it has 0 A, and it ends.

00:15:42.869 --> 00:15:45.869
Now, we have ha. Ha is true because it has H at the beginning, A

00:15:49.851 --> 00:15:54.851
at least once and there is the end of it. Then we have ha. This is also true, because I said that I'm looking for a string which has

00:15:59.526 --> 00:16:02.526
zero or more A's, and ba is false, because ba doesn't start with H.

00:16:02.972 --> 00:16:06.972
Now, let us look at the example with +.

00:16:09.766 --> 00:16:13.766
So plus one or more times. So, now the first element here is false. Why? Because

00:16:17.831 --> 00:16:19.831
plus indicates that we need to have A at least once.

00:16:24.211 --> 00:16:26.211
And here in h, we don't have any. But it's true for ha, because we have

00:16:26.750 --> 00:16:33.750
H and A at least once, and it's true for haaa, because we have h as the first element

00:16:40.466 --> 00:16:43.466
and three A's, which is under the requested condition to be one or more time.

00:16:53.114 --> 00:16:56.114
Now the question mark: zero or one time. Now, we look at this pattern color, and then, what does grepl() give us? So, in color

00:17:10.189 --> 00:17:12.189
we have true. Why? Because we need to have something that starts with the C-O-L-O, and then U should be there 0 or 1 time. So, actually, here is 0 time. This is true.

00:17:15.070 --> 00:17:19.070
Now, we have colour with U. And it's also true, because we ask

00:17:23.828 --> 00:17:26.828
to have in our pattern one or zero times, so this is true.

00:17:31.644 --> 00:17:34.644
But it's false in colouur, because we have U two times.

00:17:34.685 --> 00:17:39.685
And in curly brackets, at least M and at

00:17:42.118 --> 00:17:44.118
most N times, so we have this a.

00:17:50.191 --> 00:17:53.191
Okay, so the first element is false. Why? Because A appears only once, and we need to have

00:17:56.247 --> 00:17:58.247
A at least 2, and at most N times.

00:17:59.067 --> 00:18:05.067
It's true for aa. Why? Because we have A 2 times, and we said that we wanted to have A

00:18:09.865 --> 00:18:11.865
between 2 and 4 times. It's true for the third example, because we have three A's,

00:18:17.736 --> 00:18:21.736
and it's true for the fourth example, because we have 4 A's, but it's false for the

00:18:20.955 --> 00:18:24.955
last example, because we have 5 A's, and we ask only to have

00:18:31.279 --> 00:18:33.279
at most four. Okay, and if we go back to our poem, we are looking for joy or love.

00:18:37.344 --> 00:18:39.344
And then, indeed, as an output, that would match joy and love

00:18:44.942 --> 00:18:47.942
and then here, we are looking for something with i and lowercase letter at least

00:18:52.222 --> 00:18:57.222
twice. So, we have ing in thing, we have increases, and we have

00:19:03.595 --> 00:19:07.595
ing in nothingness, etc. And then, just

00:19:10.906 --> 00:19:14.906
a tiny bit of an additional info. So here we have these further characters to match

00:19:14.348 --> 00:19:21.348
any meta character, and then for that, we use a backslash. For example \. matches the dot.

00:19:21.577 --> 00:19:27.577
We have also a few special characters, so we have this \t, it matches tab,

00:19:27.840 --> 00:19:32.840
\n, a new line, \v, vertical tab, etc.

00:19:32.228 --> 00:19:40.228
And then, we also have character sequences. So character sequences here, any character sequences between A and Z,

00:19:42.849 --> 00:19:45.849
that matches all lowercase letters, and A and F matches

00:19:54.338 --> 00:19:57.338
A till F, for example. And then, to sum it up, I will just show you this table, but don't be concerned, because this is just like an overview, so

00:19:56.768 --> 00:20:00.768
what I said at the very beginning, that there are different character classes,

00:20:06.221 --> 00:20:09.221
and here you can see that sometimes I can use different ways to ask for the same thing. So, for example, if I would like to ask for a space

00:20:14.696 --> 00:20:17.696
and a tab, I can either write blank between two columns, or I could write

00:20:22.791 --> 00:20:24.791
space, and a tab. Then, for example for digits, we have 3 types to write it, so either I use this first

00:20:25.777 --> 00:20:30.777
style, then I could also choose the second style with D and a backslash, or

00:20:35.866 --> 00:20:38.866
0 and 9. They are all supported. And then it's up to your personal preference what to choose.

00:20:38.425 --> 00:20:48.425
They all should work fine in R. Okay, so let me stop here, and then we are going to come back to even more examples.

